fractional-stability-uncertainty-ce19769.interpretation:
In precision timekeeping and metrology, $
u(t)$ represents the instantaneous frequency generated by an oscillator, and $
u_0$ represents its designated nominal frequency. The quantity $y(t) =  rac{
u(t) - 
u_0}{
u_0}$ quantifies the instantaneous fractional drift or noise. Time-averaged statistics of $y(t)$, such as the Allan variance $\sigma_y^2(	au)$ or modified Allan variance, characterize the stability and systematic noise profiles (e.g., white frequency noise, flicker noise, random walk) over varying observation intervals $	au$.
